===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# [[hesitation|Hesitation]] to act from the difficulty of determining what is [[right#Adjective|right]] or [[expedient]]; [[doubt#Noun|doubt]], [[hesitation]] or [[unwillingness]] due to [[motive#Noun|motives]] of [[conscience]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Geo[rge] Chapman|authorlink=George Chapman|chapter=Conclusio|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=epr7b0_O0LEC&pg=PP43|title=Evthymiæ Raptvs; or The Teares of Peace: With Interlocutions|location=London|publisher=Printed by H. L. for Rich[ard] Bonian, and H. Walley,  and are to be solde at the spread-eagle, neere the great North-door of [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|S. Pauls Church]]|year=1609|oclc=53703972|passage=Before her flew Affliction, girt in ſtorms, / Gaſht all with guſhing wounds; and all the formes / Of bane, and miſerie, frowning in her face; / Whom Tyrannie, and Iniuſtice, had in Chace; / Grimme Perſecution, Pouertie, and Shame; / Detraction, Enuie, foule Mishap and lame; / '''Scruple''' of Conſcience; Feare, Deceipt, Deſpaire; / Slaunder, and Clamor, that rent all the Ayre; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=David Jardine|authorlink=David Jardine (barrister)|title=A Reading on the Use of Torture in the Criminal Law of England previously to the Commonwealth; Delivered at {{w|New Inn Hall}} in Michaelmas Term, 1836, by Appointment of the [[w:Middle Temple|Honourable Society of the Middle Temple]]|location=London|publisher=Baldwin and Cradock|year=1837|page=16|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Uz1fAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA16|oclc=156119483|passage=[U]ntil the [[w:Commonwealth of England|Commonwealth [of England]]] torture was constantly used as an instrument of evidence in the investigation of offences, whether municipal or political, without '''scruple''', and without question as to its legality.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas Babington Macaulay|authorlink=Thomas Babington Macaulay|chapter=John Bunyan|title=Biographical Essays. [...] Frederic the Great.—Bunyan.—Goldsmith.—Johnson.—Barère|series=Collection of British Authors|seriesvolume=CCCCV|edition=Tauchniz|location=Leipzig|publisher=[[w:Tauchnitz publishers|Bernhard Tauchniz]]|year=1857|page=99|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jabYNNVDK7YC&pg=PA99|oclc=909866528|passage=The four chief sins of which he was guilty were dancing, ringing the bells of the parish church, playing at tipcat, and reading the History of Sir [[w:Bevis of Hampton|Bevis of Southampton]]. A Rector of the school of [[w:William Laud|[William] Laud]] would have held such a young man up to the whole parish as a model. But [[w:John Bunyan|[John] Bunyan]]'s notions of good and evil had been learned in a very different school; and he was made miserable by the conflict between his tastes and his '''scruples'''.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1942|author={{w|Julius J. Epstein}}; {{w|Philip G. Epstein}}; {{w|Howard Koch (screenwriter)|Howard Koch}}|title={{w|Casablanca (film)|Casablanca}}|role=Captain Renault|actor=Claude Rains|passage=Ricky, I'm going to miss you. Apparently you're the only one in Casablanca with less '''scruples''' than I.}}
# {{lb|en|pharmacy}} A [[weight]] of {{frac|1|288}} of a pound, that is, twenty [[grain]]s or one third of a [[dram]], about 1.3 [[gram]]s (symbol: [[℈]]).
#: {{syn|en|s.ap.|q1=abbreviation}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Leonardo Fioravanti|authorlink=Leonardo Fioravanti (doctor)|author2=John Hester, transl.|chapter=To Make Oyle of Frankensence|title=A Short Discours of the Excellent Doctour and Knight, Maister Leonardo Phioravanti Bolognese vppon Chirurgerie. With a Declaration of Many Thinges, Necessarie to be Knowne, neuer Written before in this Order: Whereunto is Added a Number of Notable Secretes, Found out by the saide Author. Translated out of Italyan into English, by Iohn Hester, Practicioner in the Arte of Distillation|location=London|publisher=Imprinted at London by {{w|Thomas East}}|year=1580|section=folio 17, recto|sectionurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hf81AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA17|oclc=309029869|passage=The Oyle ſerueth in many operations, and ſpecially in all colde diſeaſes, if they be inwardly, give thereof euerye morning one '''ſcruple''' to drinke, and if they be outward annoynt.}}
#* {{RQ:Dictionaire Oeconomique|entry=BEZOAR-STONE|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iXIiAQAAMAAJ&pg=PP102|passage=''Bezoar'' (''Horſe'') called ''Hypolites'', excites Sweat, reſiſts Poiſon, kills the Worms, and ſtops a Looſeneſs; the Doſe is from half a '''Scruple''' to two '''Scruples'''.}}
# A [[Hebrew]] [[unit]] of [[time#Noun|time]] [[equal]] to {{frac|1080}} [[hour]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Giles Strauchius [''i.e.'', {{w|Aegidius Strauch II}}]|author2={{w|Richard Sault}}, transl.|chapter=Of Minutes, Scruples and Moments|title=Breviarium Chronologicum. Or A Treatise Describing the Terms and Most Celebrated Characters, Periods and Epocha’s Used in Chronology. By Giles Strauchius, D.D. and Publick Professor in the University of Wittebergh. Now Done into English from the Third Edition, in Latin. By Richard Sault, F.R.S.|edition=2nd corrected|location=London|publisher=Printed for A. Bosvile at the Dial and Bible against [[w:St Dunstan-in-the-West|St. Dunstan's Church]] in [[w:Fleet Street|Fleet-street]]|year=1704|section=book I (Of some Terms in Chronology, and those the Most Common), §&nbsp;3|page=8|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pSkIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA8|oclc=912637911|passage=If it be ask'd why the Jews divide the Hour into 1080 '''Scruples''', the Author of the ''Neomeniæ'' ſet forth by ''Munſter'' gives this reaſon fo it, becauſe there is no number that is diviſible into ſo many ſorts as this of 1080 is; for it may be divided by 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 15, 20, &c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas Watson|chapter=Chronology|title=An Useful Compendium of Many Important and Curious Branches of Science and General Knowledge, Digested, Principally, in Plain and Instructive Tables, to which are Added, some Rational Recreations in Numbers, with Easy and Expeditious Methods of Constructing Magic Squares, and Specimens of Some in the Higher Class|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:Longman|Longman, Hurst, Rees, Orme and Brown]], {{w|Paternoster Row}}; C. Law, [[w:Ave Maria Lane|Ave-Maria Lane]]; by and for R. Rodgers, Whitby|year=1812|section=section 4 (The Mahometan Year)|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fcNCAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA38|oclc=892308771|passage=The Mahometan year consists of 12 lunar months, each containing 29 days, 12 hours and 792 '''scruples'''; (1080 '''scruples''' = 1 hour) so that the year contains 354 days, 8 hours and 864 '''scruples'''.|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=George Greenwood|title=East of the Jordan|magazine=[[w:The Athenaeum (British magazine)|The Athenæum: Journal of English and Foreign Literature, Science, and the Fine Arts]]|location=London|publisher=Printed by James Holmes, Took's Court, {{w|Chancery Lane}}, published at the office, 20, Wellington Street, [[w:Strand, London|Strand]], W.C., by John Francis. [...]|date=23 July 1864|issue=1917|page=116|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=QpIeAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA116|column=1|oclc=956082422|passage=The most ancient hour was divided into 1,080 '''scruples'''. The Jews suppose {{w|Issachar}} to have brought it from heaven.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|by extension}} A very small quantity; a [[particle]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:modicum}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William Shakespeare|authorlink=William Shakespeare|chapter=[[w:Twelfth Night|Tvvelfe Night, or What You Will]]|title=[[w:First Folio|Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies: Published According to the True Originall Copies]]|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:William Jaggard|Isaac Iaggard]], and [[w:Edward Blount|Ed[ward] Blount]]|year=c. 1600–1601|year_published=1623|section=Act III, scene iv|page=267, column 2|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uNtBAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA267|oclc=606515358|passage=Why euery thing adheres togither, that no dramme of a '''ſcruple''', no '''ſcruple''' of a '''ſcruple''', no obſtacle, no incredulous or unſafe circumſtance: What can be ſaide? Nothing that can be, can come betweene me, and the full proſpect of my hopes.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[doubt#Noun|doubt]] or [[uncertainty]] concerning a matter of fact; [[intellectual]] [[perplexity]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William Okeley|chapter=Sect. VIII. The Contrivance for Our Escape, the Persons Acquainted with It, and also Those that were Engaged in it; Some Debates about Leaving My Patron.|title=Eben-ezer: Or, A Small Monument of Great Mercy, Appearing in the Miraculous Deliverance of William Okeley, William Adams, John Anthony, John Jephs, John— Carpenter, from the Miserable Slavery of Algiers, [...]|location=London|publisher=Printed for Nat[haniel] Ponder, at the sign of the Peacock in the [[w:Poultry, London|Poultry]] near [[w:Cornhill, London|Corn-hill]], and in [[w:Chancery Lane|Chancery-Lane]] near [[w:Fleet Street|Fleetstreet]]|year=1676|page=46|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=HSwLAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA47|oclc=81479015|passage=There aroſe ''a '''Scruple''''', nay, it amount to ''a Queſtion'', whether to attempt an Eſcape from my Patron, one that ''ſo dearly Loved'' me, ''ſo fairly bought me'', were juſtifiable before God and Men?}}

